<h3 id="so-widgets-bundle">Google Maps API Key</h3>

<p>
	The <strong>SiteOrigin Google Maps</strong> widget from the SiteOrigin Widgets Bundle plugin needs a Google Maps API key. Until you add one, its maps show an error, including the maps on imported demo pages. One key works for every map on your site.
</p>
<ol>
	<li>Follow Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/get-api-key" target="_blank">Maps API key guide</a> to create a project in Google Cloud and an API key. Google asks you to set up a billing account for Maps, even if your usage stays within its free monthly allowance.</li>
	<li>In the same project, enable the <strong>Maps JavaScript API</strong> and the <strong>Geocoding API</strong>, which finds the map center from the address you enter. The address suggestions in the widget's <strong>Map center</strong> field also need the <strong>Places API</strong>. If you set a map's type to <strong>Static image</strong>, also enable the <strong>Maps Static API</strong>.</li>
	<li>Restrict the key to your website's address, as the guide describes, so nobody else can use it.</li>
	<li>In WordPress, go to <strong>Plugins &gt; SiteOrigin Widgets</strong>, find <strong>Google Maps</strong> and click <strong>Settings</strong>. Paste the key into <strong>API key</strong> and click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>
	Older versions of the plugin had an API key field in each map widget. That field is gone: the key in the plugin settings is used for all maps.
</p>
